The issue: my get_arc_tail returns the last 8 frames of the last arc (frames 409-416), but in this case the ball is rising within those frames. So the vy is positive.

This means my apex detection is too coarse. The actual juggling has more bounces than 5 in 415 frames (which is 14 sec at 30fps; for 3-ball cascade, that's ~10 throws in 14 sec, so 5 apexes is reasonable). But within each arc, the ball may not be monotonically falling — it could be rising if the tracklet includes the catch-throw-rethrow motion.

OK so v6 doesn't really help. The fundamental problem is that long tracklets in YouTube contain MANY bouncing cycles, and any naive "tail/head" of a long tracklet is unlikely to represent a single parabolic arc.

Let me give up on v6 and instead make H8 v6 a documented NEGATIVE result (per-bounce segmentation doesn't help on YouTube because the apex detection itself is too coarse). I'll write a short report.
